# `k = &f1 (id 11)` hands f1 out as a value holding a subtype of int, and
# `f1 16 (id 19)` is the only direct call. Inference read f1's first
# parameter from the direct call alone, so f1 was compiled for an int there,
# and the held subtype came back out as its box's address. A partial now
# widens its group's parameters, as a bare mention of the group does.
type id int

fn f1 p2 p3
  return p2 if 20 > p3
  p3 + 1

fn go z
  k = &f1 (id 11)
  a = k (z - z)
  b = f1 16 (id 19)
  "{a} {b}"

pub play = print "{go 5}"
